<article class="jd-rulebook">
  <header class="jd-rulebook__title">
    <span class="jd-kicker">Ghost stories in the manner of M. R. James</span>
    <h1>Jamesian Dark</h1>
    <p>A one-page hack of Graham Walmsley's <em>Cthulhu Dark</em>. You play bookish people who disturb something that should have been left alone. You cannot defeat it; you can learn what it is, try to lay it to rest, or get away.</p>
  </header>

  <h2>Making an Investigator</h2>
  <p>Choose a name, an occupation and an area of scholarly interest: the thing that gets you into trouble. Notice starts at 1. Play one to three Investigators.</p>

  <h2>The dice</h2>
  <p>When the outcome is uncertain, build a pool and read the highest die.</p>
  <ul class="jd-rulebook__dice">
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--light" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> One Light die if the action is within human capability.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--scholar" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> One Light die if it falls within your occupation or scholarly interest.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--notice" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> The Notice die if you risk your mind.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--meddle" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> The Meddle die if you meddle.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If the Notice die is highest, make a Notice roll after the result.</p>

  <div class="jd-rulebook__tables">
    <table>
      <caption>Investigating</caption>
      <tr><th>1–3</th><td>The bare minimum you need to go on.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>4</th><td>What a competent scholar would find.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>5</th><td>More: a parallel case, a remembered footnote, a local story.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>6</th><td>A glimpse of what is really happening. Make a Notice roll.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <table>
      <caption>Doing anything else</caption>
      <tr><th>1–3</th><td>Fail, or succeed at a cost the GM names.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>4</th><td>Succeed.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>5</th><td>Succeed well.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>6</th><td>Succeed, and notice something wrong. The GM may call for a Notice roll.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice</h2>
  <p>Notice runs from 1 to 6 and measures how far the thing has noticed you. It is your only track.</p>
  <p><strong>Notice roll.</strong> When you see something wrong, or your Notice die comes up highest, roll a die. If it beats your current Notice, Notice rises by 1. Roleplay the unease.</p>
  <p>Notice falls only if you leave the story.</p>

  <h2>Meddling</h2>
  <p>Meddling is the tempting shortcut every James scholar takes. Add the Meddle die to your roll: it makes success likelier, and it may be what gives you away.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If the Meddle die is highest, or ties for highest, Notice rises by 1 automatically.</li>
    <li>If another die beats it, the meddling went unremarked.</li>
    <li>If the Meddle die and Notice die tie for highest, Notice rises by 1, then make a Notice roll.</li>
    <li>Each distinct transgression counts once.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What counts as meddling</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Taking something away from where it was left.</li>
    <li>Reading words aloud, or using an object for its purpose.</li>
    <li>Opening what was sealed, or disturbing the dead.</li>
    <li>Going where you were warned not to, or ignoring a warning.</li>
    <li>Sleeping in, staying in, or returning to the wrong place.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Keeping it legible.</strong> The GM may offer a bargain: “You could prise the lid off — that's meddling.” Players may always ask whether an action counts, and the GM answers honestly. Players may know the cost when their characters don't; the reader always knows not to blow the whistle.</p>

  <h2>Solitude and the Visit</h2>
  <p>The thing manifests directly only to someone who is alone. A smell, a sound or a far-off figure can reach anyone; a hand on the bedroom door cannot. Company is safe, so splitting up is always a choice.</p>
  <p><strong>The Visit.</strong> At Notice 6, the next time you are alone, it comes for you. The table plays the scene out. You cannot win it; you may run, hide or endure.</p>

  <h2>Failing and fighting</h2>
  <p><strong>The Failure Die.</strong> If anyone thinks it would be more interesting for you to fail at something other than investigating, they describe how and roll a die. If it beats your highest die, you fail as they described.</p>
  <p><strong>Trying again.</strong> Unhappy with a roll? If you didn't roll the Notice die, add it and reroll. If you did, make any Notice roll, then reroll everything.</p>
  <p><strong>No fighting.</strong> You cannot harm the thing. If you try, your Notice goes straight to 6.</p>

  <h2>Endings</h2>
  <p><strong>Laying it to rest.</strong> The only true victory is restitution: return the object, finish the interrupted rite, give back what was taken. Finding out how is the investigation.</p>
  <p><strong>Leaving.</strong> You may get out at any time: flee the district, burn the papers, swear never to speak of it. Roll a die; if it is lower than your Notice, Notice falls by 1. You are then out of the story and go to your epilogue.</p>
  <p><strong>Epilogue.</strong> When the story ends, each character's Notice sets their coda.</p>
  <table class="jd-rulebook__epilogue">
    <tr><th>1–2</th><td>Untouched. Perhaps a little quieter at dinner.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>3–4</th><td>Never quite well again. Will not discuss it; sleeps with a light on.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>5–6</th><td>Ruined: gone abroad, hair turned white, or found in the morning.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Anyone who suffered the Visit takes the 5–6 epilogue, whatever their final Notice.</p>

  {{#if isGM}}
  <h2>For the GM</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One transgression, one thing.</strong> Build each story around a transgression already made and a single entity tethered to an object or place.</li>
    <li><strong>Seed warnings.</strong> An inscription, a sexton's remark, a note in a margin, locals who won't come up the lane. Ignoring one is meddling.</li>
    <li><strong>Six Provenance discoveries.</strong> Letters, registers, engravings, a diary, the object itself. Every essential clue must be reachable without meddling; meddling gets there faster, or further.</li>
    <li><strong>Escalate slowly.</strong> Unease (a small wrongness), dread (something specific), terror (alone, and it is near), horror (the thing in the room, once per story).</li>
    <li><strong>Stay reticent.</strong> Never describe it whole: a hand, damp linen, hair, the wrong number of limbs felt in the dark.</li>
    <li><strong>Bracket the horror with comfort.</strong> Sherry, fussy antiquarian dispute, dry humour. Company is safe, so let the players enjoy it.</li>
    <li><strong>Offer a bargain</strong> when the table is cautious and the story slows.</li>
    <li><strong>Optional frame.</strong> Open with a narrator, years later: “My friend never spoke of what happened at Burnstow…”</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}
